@page "/resources"
@inject ChallengeConfig Config

<PageTitle>Resources &amp; FAQ — Santa's Workshop</PageTitle>

<div class="wrap">
    <h1>Resources &amp; FAQ</h1>

    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>

    <div class="faq">
        <details open>
            <summary>Do I have to do the exercises to claim a badge?</summary>
            <p>
                No. The badge assessments are open to anyone, free, and you can take
                any of them whenever you like — the links are right there on
                <a href="parts">the challenges page</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                What the exercises give you is the practice. The badges test the
                concepts these parts teach, so doing the part first tends to make the
                assessment a formality rather than a guess.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Do I need to know MongoDB already?</summary>
            <p>
                No. Part 1 starts at <code>InsertOne()</code> and assumes nothing. It
                does assume you're comfortable in C# — you'll be writing methods, not
                copying files around.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you're coming from SQL, the Part 1 README has collapsible boxes that
                translate each concept from the relational world as you go.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Is this a daily advent calendar? Will I fall behind?</summary>
            <p>
                No, and no. Parts are released through December, but it's
                self-paced and <strong>evergreen</strong> — do it in February if that
                suits you better. Nothing expires and nothing is scored.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How long does each part take?</summary>
            <p>
                About <strong>30 to 60 minutes</strong> for the core path. Each part
                also has a <em>Level up</em> section that goes deeper; that's genuinely
                optional and isn't counted in the time.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Do I need a credit card, or a paid MongoDB account?</summary>
            <p>
                No. The local Docker setup needs no account at all, and the Atlas free
                tier needs no card. The two AI services used in Parts 3B and 4 — Voyage
                AI and Google Gemini — both have free tiers that don't ask for one either.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Can I do the parts out of order?</summary>
            <p>
                Mostly. The dataset grows as you go and later parts assume the earlier
                concepts, but each part ships with its own seed data, so jumping
                straight to Part 3B will work. If you're new to MongoDB, in order is
                much less effort.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>I'm stuck on a step. Is using SOLUTIONS.md cheating?</summary>
            <p>
                No. Every part has a <code>SOLUTIONS.md</code> with the answer to each
                step, plus an explanation of <em>why</em> and the alternative ways of
                writing it. Read it, then come back and write it yourself.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Why C#, when most MongoDB material is JavaScript or Python?</summary>
            <p>
                Exactly that. The badge assessments mostly use MongoDB Shell syntax, so
                each part ends with a <em>"From C# to the badge"</em> table translating
                what you wrote into what the assessment will show you.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Something is broken. Where do I go?</summary>
            <p>
                Run <code>dotnet run --project tools/doctor</code> first — it diagnoses
                most problems and tells you the fix. After that, the
                <a href="setup#troubleshooting">troubleshooting section</a>,
                and then
                <a href="@(Config.Repo)/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue</a>.
                If a step confused you, that's a bug in the material and worth reporting.
            </p>
        </details>
    </div>

    <h2>Useful links</h2>

    <ul class="links">
        @foreach (var resource in Config.Resources)
        {
            <li>
                <a href="@resource.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@resource.Name ↗</a>
                <span>@resource.Note</span>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    <h2>Inside the repo</h2>

    <ul class="links">
        <li>
            <a href="setup">Setup guide</a>
            <span>Full setup instructions for all three routes, plus troubleshooting.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="docs/csharp-to-badge">From C# to the badge</a>
            <span>C# ↔ shell, Compass and Atlas UI translation tables for every part.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="docs/objective-map">Objective map</a>
            <span>Every badge objective, mapped to the step that teaches it and the test that proves it.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="@(Config.Repo)/blob/main/DECISIONS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DECISIONS.md ↗</a>
            <span>Why things are built the way they are — including the bits that surprised us.</span>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>Found a mistake, or a step that didn't make sense?</strong>
            <a href="@(Config.Repo)/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>.
            Confusing explanations are bugs.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
